{"seq":1,"at":"2026-09-17","kind":"imported","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0262"},"reason":"Article of the Quiztopia bank (written for card c022): researched and transcribed in September 2026, converted to the quiz platform on 2026-10-20.","by":"Quiztopia import","ref":"quiz-archive/transcripts/quiztopia/c022.json"},
{"seq":2,"at":"2026-10-20","kind":"edited","target":{"type":"article","id":"a0262"},"reason":"Rewritten by the merge to take in the printed cards' questions.","by":"merge geography/physical, geography/physical","ref":"quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/geography__physical.g1.json quiz-archive/runs/tp-ingest/decisions/zz-r2-geography__physical.json","changes":[{"field":"bodyEn","diff":[["=",574],["+","The smallest of the well-known German islands makes the strangest rules. Heligoland, Germany's only island on the open sea, about 60 kilometres off the coast, measures barely one square kilometre, and on its narrow lanes cars and even bicycles are banned under the road traffic regulations. Only people with a special permit may ride, and since 1 October 2018 children up to the age of 14 may cycle there in the winter months, from October to April, when the day-trippers have gone.\n\n"],["=",204],["+","The Mediterranean's two largest islands both belong to Italy – Sicily and Sardinia, about 25,400 and 23,800 square kilometres – ahead of Cyprus, Corsica and Crete. "],["=",283]]},{"field":"bodyDe","diff":[["=",494],["+","Die kleinste der bekannten deutschen Inseln hat die seltsamsten Regeln. Helgoland, Deutschlands einzige Hochseeinsel, rund 60 Kilometer vor der Küste, ist kaum einen Quadratkilometer groß, und auf ihren schmalen Wegen sind nach der Straßenverkehrs-Ordnung Autos und sogar Fahrräder verboten. Radfahren darf nur, wer eine Sondergenehmigung hat, und seit dem 1. Oktober 2018 dürfen Kinder bis 14 Jahre dort in der Winterzeit, von Oktober bis April, wenn die Tagesgäste fort sind, Rad fahren.\n\n"],["=",188],["+","Die beiden größten Inseln des Mittelmeers gehören beide zu Italien – Sizilien und Sardinien, rund 25.400 und 23.800 Quadratkilometer groß – vor Zypern, Korsika und Kreta. "],["=",261]]}]},
